s. The 20k's early slips (F8, G6, F5, F4 each lost 4.1 to 4.6 points) built up a lead of 11.3 points for White by move 15. Quieter White moves later, E6, D3 and D5, gave back most of it, but the final analysis still had White ahead by 3.9, enough to carry komi 7.", "moments": [{"turn": 5, "text": "Black's F8 lost 4.1 points and moved the lead from -0.1 to -4.2, against the engine's D6. It was the first big drop, and the game never got back to even."}, {"turn": 10, "text": "GPT-6.1 Sol's E4 joined the E5 and E3 stones into one column, but it was the worst White move of the game at 4.9 points lost (-9.2 to -4.3). The engine preferred F4, the very point Black took next."}, {"turn": 11, "text": "Black's F4 lost 4.6 points, the 20k's worst move, sending the lead from -4.3 to -9.0. The F column now ran down the board as a wall facing White's E column."}, {"turn": 13, "text": "Black's E2 lost 4.5 points (-5.8 to -10.2); the engine wanted F2. Black pushed under White's group on the second line, and the analysis had White's lead past ten by move 15, peaking at -11.3."}, {"turn": 28, "text": "GPT-6.1 Sol's D5 lost 1.9 points (-5.8 to -3.8) and the engine preferred F7. It still sealed a row of White stones along the fifth line, A5 to E5, and from there the lead held at about 3.9 to the end."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "Every script either called the go/file tools, stored raw responses, or ran game-114-inspection.js (parsing the board, groups, liberties and regions, area counts) and game-114-verification.js (replaying only the actual recorded moves to check legality and snapshot matches and to build the SGF). Nothing looped over empty points to score candidates or played hypothetical continuations; the moves were chosen by hand and each play call carried a single named move.", "stop": false}}